# 7b-2 (ticket OPUS-7B2-20260929-01; L2 §10, §18.2; book §12 keep(3) = 4): a formal becomes graph-backed only by an
# explicit same-name declaration, and from there the name is that declared field under the working state.  `int: n`
# takes the argument's current value 3, `n: n + 1` makes the working value 4 and marks it; nop() publishes it.
# poke() writes 100 into the place of declaration from another method; the second nop() publishes nothing (the working
# value is clean); peek() reads 100 -- while the bare n keeps 4 (nothing is reloaded).  n * 1000 + peeked = 4100.
# Observed from other methods only, so carry is walked under the knob.  In place (the name is the cell): 100100; a
# carry into the cell alone (the working value keeps the entry load): 1100.  Success is 7.
fn: nop () int
    return: 0
end: nop
fn: carry (int: n) int
    int: n
    n: n + 1
    nop()
    poke()
    nop()
    int: g 0
    g: peek()
    return: n * 1000 + g
end: carry
fn: poke () int
    carry\n: 100
    return: 0
end: poke
fn: peek () int
    return: carry\n
end: peek
int: r 0
r: carry(3)
int: ok 64
if: r = 4100
    ok: 7
if: r = 100100
    ok: 65
if: r = 1100
    ok: 66
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
